    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that. One thing I'm worried about is the sheer messiness of these items—some are just loose papers or fragments from different eras that don't clearly fit a category. When things are that disorganized and don't seem to follow any logical sequence, how do these systems help prevent the collection from becoming a total chaotic heap?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC) utilizes decimal numbers for classification; very detailed subdivisions allow exceptional precision and cross-reference capabilities. Unfortunately, this can become cumbersome for your scattered manuscripts, as subsequent revisions could lead to an unwieldy system. The Library of Congress Classification (LCC), with its use of moveable alphabetical characters and numbers, offers greater flexibility in cataloging irregular or mixed-genre items. Librarians can use non-standard call numbers or create new ones if the existing system doesn't suffice. This adaptability helps in categorizing a disorganized collection systematically without enforcing rigid structure, thus allowing individual items to be described in the metadata with more nuanced details, increasing their usability for researchers despite chaotic initial appearances.",
